issue.body:
When validating these lines of javascript:

``` javascript
var func = function (first, ...rest) {
  return rest
}

func(1, 2, 3)
```

with this .eslintrc config:

``` javascript
{
    "rules": {
        "strict": false,
        "semi": 0
     },
    "ecmaFeatures": {
        "restParams": true,
        "spread": true
    }
}
```

I still get the following errors:

```
restParams.js
  1:21  error  first is defined but never used  no-unused-vars
  2:9   error  'rest' is not defined        no-undef

✖ 2 problems (2 errors, 0 warnings)
```

The rule doesn't seem to realize that there is a second parameter named 'rest' in the function declaration.

``` shell
$ eslint --version
v0.16.1
```

.eslintrc

``` json
{
  "env": {
    "es6": true
  },
  "rules": {
    "block-scoped-var": 2
  },
  "ecmaFeatures": {
    "destructuring": true
  }
}
```

test.js

``` js
'use strict';

var fun = function({x}) {
  return x;
};

var fun2 = function(arg) {
  var {x} = arg;
  return x;
};

fun({x: 2});
fun2({x: 2});
```

Error

```
test.js
  4:9  error  "x" used outside of binding context  block-scoped-var

✖ 1 problem (1 error, 0 warnings)
```

As you can see, the second function is linted correctly (due to #1863), but the first one fails.

